From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: SHGetKnownFolderPath accepts KF_FLAG_NO_APPCONTAINER_REDIRECTION

Real Microsoft software, run as itself, asks for a known folder with
KF_FLAG_NO_APPCONTAINER_REDIRECTION (0x00010000) -- "give me the true folder,
not the AppContainer's private redirect of it." winget (WindowsPackageManager.dll)
does exactly this while resolving its settings path: it calls
SHGetKnownFolderPath with flags 0x00015000
(NO_APPCONTAINER_REDIRECTION | DONT_VERIFY | NO_ALIAS).

Wine's SHGetKnownFolderPath rejects any flag outside a fixed whitelist, and that
whitelist omitted KF_FLAG_NO_APPCONTAINER_REDIRECTION -- even though the value is
defined in <shlobj.h>. So the call returned E_INVALIDARG. winget wraps the call
in wil's THROW_IF_FAILED, so the failure became a C++ exception that unwound
through a noexcept boundary and terminated the process before it could print
anything: `winget --version` died with no output.

Wine has no AppContainer and performs no per-container redirection, so
"do not redirect into the AppContainer" is already how every path is resolved:
honouring the flag is a no-op. Add it to the accepted mask, so the folder is
returned normally instead of the call failing. Any process that passes it --
which every packaged Microsoft app does -- now gets its folder rather than an
error. Behaviour for callers that never set the flag is unchanged.

--- a/dlls/shell32/shellpath.c
+++ b/dlls/shell32/shellpath.c
@@ -3538,7 +3538,8 @@ HRESULT WINAPI SHGetKnownFolderPath(REFKNOWNFOLDERID rfid, DWORD flags, HANDLE to
         return HRESULT_FROM_WIN32( ERROR_FILE_NOT_FOUND );
 
     if (flags & ~(KF_FLAG_CREATE|KF_FLAG_SIMPLE_IDLIST|KF_FLAG_DONT_UNEXPAND|
-        KF_FLAG_DONT_VERIFY|KF_FLAG_NO_ALIAS|KF_FLAG_INIT|KF_FLAG_DEFAULT_PATH|KF_FLAG_NOT_PARENT_RELATIVE))
+        KF_FLAG_DONT_VERIFY|KF_FLAG_NO_ALIAS|KF_FLAG_INIT|KF_FLAG_DEFAULT_PATH|KF_FLAG_NOT_PARENT_RELATIVE|
+        KF_FLAG_NO_APPCONTAINER_REDIRECTION))
     {
         FIXME("flags 0x%08lx not supported\n", flags);
         return E_INVALIDARG;
